---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import benchmarking from '../../assets/images/ux-benchmarking.png';
import journeyMap1 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-1.png';
---
<Layout title="Benchmarking | Andrew Smith" description="Quantitative UX benchmarking, measuring effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability to track whether digital products are improving over time.">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main id="main-content">
      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/portfolio/work">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator">/</span><span>Benchmarking</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">Measuring whether digital products are actually getting better.</h1>
        <p class="hero-text">Most organisations know their product has problems. What they do not always know is whether the changes they are making are improving things or just changing them. Analytics data shows what users are doing. It does not explain why they are doing it, how confident they feel, or whether the experience is getting easier or harder over time.</p>
        <p class="hero-text">Quantitative UX benchmarking fills that gap. It measures the experience consistently, across the same four dimensions, at regular intervals, so the data compounds over time and tells a story about direction, not just current state. When a product ships a major change, the next benchmarking study either validates that the change worked or reveals where it introduced new friction. Either outcome is valuable.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="meta-strip">
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Insurance, member portal, mobile app</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">Senior UX Design Lead</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">UserTesting.com, Optimal Workshop, analytics</p></div>
      </section>
      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={benchmarking} alt="Usability benchmarking dashboard showing effectiveness 39/1, efficiency 4 minute average, satisfaction scores, and desirability word cloud" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>
      <section class="summary-section">
        <p class="section-kicker">The Four Dimensions</p>
        <h2>Effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability , each with a different job to do.</h2>
        <div class="summary-main">
          <p>Effectiveness asks whether users can complete the task at all. A product with high effectiveness but low efficiency is technically usable but exhausting to use. A product with high efficiency but low satisfaction might be quick, but users do not trust it. Desirability captures the emotional quality of the experience: the words users choose to describe it reveal whether it feels professional, approachable, clear, or cluttered.</p>
          <p>Running these four dimensions together paints a complete picture. A drop in desirability scores while effectiveness holds steady points to a different problem than a drop in efficiency with stable satisfaction. The data tells you not just that something is wrong, but what kind of thing is wrong and where to look first.</p>
          <p>These studies also integrate with NPS, CSAT, Google Analytics drop-off data, and call centre feedback, so the quantitative UX data can be interpreted alongside operational metrics rather than sitting in a separate report that nobody connects to anything else.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Connecting to Journey Maps</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>Benchmarking data does not sit in a dashboard. It feeds directly into the journey map, attaching quantitative evidence to specific stages of the experience so the team can see where the data and the research are aligned, and where they are pointing in different directions.</p>
          <p>When a drop-off rate climbs at a specific step, the journey map shows what research already exists for that stage and whether it explains the drop-off. If there is no research for that stage, the gap becomes visible immediately and feeds into the discovery backlog as a priority for the next research cycle.</p>
          <p>This is what closes the loop between measurement and action. Benchmarking without a journey map produces insights that have no home. A journey map without benchmarking data is a static artefact. Together, they create a system where evidence continuously informs what gets prioritised.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing live analytics metrics at each stage with prioritised solutions" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
.case-page{position:relative;width:min(calc(100% - 3rem),78rem);margin:0 auto;padding:1.25rem 0 5rem}
.eyebrow{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.82rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0 0 1.25rem;display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center}
.breadcrumb-link{font-weight:600}.breadcrumb-separator{color:var(--muted-foreground)}
h1,h2{margin:0;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.05em;color:var(--foreground)}
.display-title{font-size:clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3.5rem);line-height:1.06;margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:28ch}
.hero-subtext{font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--muted-foreground);font-style:italic;margin:.25rem 0 0;max-width:42rem}
.hero-text,.story-block p,.summary-main p{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 1rem}
.case-hero{padding:5rem 0 2rem}
.meta-strip{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:2rem;padding:1.5rem 0 .5rem;border-top:1px solid var(--border)}
.meta-label{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0}
.meta-value{margin:.35rem 0 0;font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700}
.lead-image-section{padding:2rem 0 4rem}
.lead-image,.supporting-image{width:100%;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:1.5rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow-large)}
.supporting-image-section{padding:2rem 0 4rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.summary-section{padding:0 0 3rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.summary-section h2{margin-top:.75rem;font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.5vw,2.2rem);max-width:32ch}
.summary-main{margin-top:1.5rem;max-width:44rem}
.section-kicker{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0 0 .5rem}
.editorial-section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:14rem minmax(0,1fr);gap:2rem;padding:3rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.story-block{max-width:44rem}
@media(max-width:860px){.meta-strip{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1rem}.editorial-section{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>
